<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mefitis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mefitis was an Italic goddess associated with sulphurous springs and vapours, waters and fertility, worshipped especially by the Samnites, Hirpini and Lucanians. Her main sanctuary lay in the Ansanto valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irpinia">Irpinia</a>, where poisonous gases rise from a small pool, and another stood at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rossano-di-vaglio">Rossano di Vaglio</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a>. The English word 'mephitic' derives from her name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mefitis.jpg" alt="Mefitis" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Redshift87 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Face_mefitis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Mefitis was worshipped by the Samnites and Osci from the 7th century BCE to the 2nd century CE, mostly in the southwest of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. She was probably first a goddess of underground sources such as springs, and because many of them were sulphurous she came to be linked with noxious gases; the 5th-century writer Servius held that any foul scent belonged to her. Modern scholars mostly reject the idea that she was a goddess of intoxication, noting that sulphurous water is found at Ampsanctus but not at her sanctuaries of Saepinum and Rossano.</p><p>Ingrid Edlund-Berry argues that she had a healing role, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> held sulphur to purify, while Allison Griffith finds no evidence that her sites served for healing or bathing. Others give her an agricultural role through her title Aravinna, perhaps from arva, soil, or see her as a mediator between the heavens and the underworld.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Her chief shrine stood at Lake Ampsanctus in Samnium, near the present village of Rocca San Felice. Cicero and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> called the lake deadly, and Pliny wrote that anyone entering her temple soon died; Servius called it the navel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Many clay votive figures from the Ansanto valley show wild boars. The site still gives off large amounts of carbon dioxide, which keeps most plants from growing there.</p><p>A temple was built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rossano-di-vaglio">Rossano di Vaglio</a> in the 4th century BCE. Its 58 inscriptions are mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> from the 4th to the 2nd century BCE and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, perhaps after the Social War. Varro mentions a grove of Mefitis by the Esquiline Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> a temple at Cremona that survived the city's destruction after the Second Battle of Bedriacum, and a wall inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> a festival in her honour organised by the gens Mamia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> linked the lake of Ampsanctus with the underworld, calling it a breathing place of savage Dis, and used the word mephitim for the deadly vapour of the grove of Albunea. Her name has been explained in many ways: from Greek words for intoxication or honey, from a Proto-Indo-European root for middle, or, as the grammarian Priscian held, from a Greek word for mediator. Finds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> statues at Rossano and shared epithets suggest links with Diana, Juno and Venus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mefitis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They lived from herding and farming in upland valleys, and the sanctuary of the goddess <b>Mefitis</b> in the Ansanto valley, by sulphurous springs, was a major cult site.</blockquote><a href="/state/hirpini">Hirpini</a></li><li><blockquote>Their federal sanctuary of the goddess <b>Mefitis</b> at Rossano di Vaglio has yielded Oscan inscriptions in Greek letters.</blockquote><a href="/state/lucanians">Lucanians</a></li></ul></section>
